WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:29.699 --> 00:00:31.589
MATHEMATICAL PRINCIPLES
OF

00:00:31.589 --> 00:00:36.000
NATURAL PHILOSOPHY
Definitions

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.960
DEFINITION I
The quantity of matter is the measure of the

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.800
same, arising from its density
and bulk conjointly.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:52.430
thus AIR of a double density, in a double
space, is quadruple in quantity; in a triple

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:57.000
space, sextuple in quantity. The same thing
is to be

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.699
understood of snow, and fine dust or powders,
that are condensed

00:01:01.699 --> 00:01:07.000
by compression or liquefaction, and of all
bodies that are by any causes

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:13.640
whatever differently condensed. I have no
regard in this place to a medium,

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:18.580
if any such there is, that freely pervades
the interstices between the parts

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:24.590
of bodies. It is this quantity that I mean
hereafter everywhere under the

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:31.220
name of body or mass. And the same is known
by the weight of each body,

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:37.149
for it is proportional to the weight, as I
have found by experiments on pendulums, very

00:01:37.149 --> 00:01:44.799
accurately made, which shall be shown hereafter.
DEFINITION II

00:01:44.799 --> 00:01:49.909
The quantity of motion 1s the measure of the
same, arising from the

00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:53.749
velocity and quantity of matter conjointly.

00:01:53.749 --> 00:01:58.729
The motion of the whole is the sum of the
motions of all the parts; and

00:01:58.729 --> 00:02:03.929
therefore in a body double in quantity, with
equal velocity, the motion is

00:02:03.929 --> 00:02:13.380
double; with twice the velocity, it is quadruple.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:14.940
DEFINITION III

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:21.500
The vis insita, or innate force of matter,
is a power of resisting, by which

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:27.680
every body, as much as in it lies, continues
in its present state, whether it

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:33.550
be of rest, or of moving uniformly forwards
in a right line.

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:38.540
This force is always proportional to the body
whose force it is and differs

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:43.540
nothing from the inactivity of the mass, but
in our manner of conceiving

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:49.920
it. A body, from the inert nature of matter,
is not without difficulty put out

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:56.849
of its state of rest or motion. Upon which
account, this vis insita may, by

00:02:56.849 --> 00:03:03.319
a most significant name, be called inertia
or force of inactivity.

00:03:03.319 --> 00:03:09.390
But a body only exerts this force when another
force, impressed upon it,

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:15.450
endeavors to change its condition; and the
exercise of this force may be considered as

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:21.860
both resistance and impulse; it is resistance
so far as the body,

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:27.390
for maintaining its present state, opposes
the force impressed; it is impulse

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:33.290
so far as the body, by not easily giving way
to the impressed force of another,

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:38.459
endeavors to change the state of that other.
Resistance is usually ascribed to

00:03:38.459 --> 00:03:45.189
bodies at rest, and impulse to those in motion;
but motion and rest, as commonly conceived,

00:03:45.189 --> 00:03:49.319
are only relatively distinguished; nor are
those bodies

00:03:49.319 --> 00:03:56.260
always truly at rest, which commonly are taken
to be so.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:04:01.079
DEFINITION IV
An impressed force is an action exerted upon

00:04:01.079 --> 00:04:06.799
a body, in order to change its
state, either of rest, or of uniform motion

00:04:06.799 --> 00:04:09.810
in aright line.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:14.920
This force consists in the action only, and
remains no longer in the body

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:20.600
when the action is over. For a body maintains
every new state it acquires,

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:26.330
by its inertia only. But impressed forces
are of different origins, as from

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:32.210
percussion, from pressure, from centripetal
force.

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:37.900
DEFINITION V
A centripetal force is that by which bodies

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:44.560
are drawn or impelled, or any
way tend, towards a point as to a centre.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:50.610
Of this sort is gravity, by which bodies tend
to the centre of the earth;

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:55.980
magnetism, by which iron tends to the loadstone;
and that force, whatever

00:04:55.980 --> 00:05:01.820
it is, by which the planets are continually
drawn aside from the rectilinear

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:08.300
motions, which otherwise they would pursue,
and made to revolve in curvilinear orbits.

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:13.600
A stone, whirled about in a sling, endeavors
to recede from the

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:19.470
hand that turns it; and by that endeavor,
distends the sling, and that with

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:25.170
so much the greater force, as it is revolved
with the greater velocity, and as

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:32.240
soon as it is let go, flies away. That force
which opposes itself to this en-

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:36.510
deavor, and by which the sling continually
draws back the stone towards

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:42.420
the hand, and retains it in its orbit, because
it is directed to the hand as the

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:48.590
centre of the orbit, I call the centripetal
force. And the same thing is to be

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:54.660
understood of all bodies, revolved in any
orbits. They all endeavor to recede

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:59.521
from the centres of their orbits; and were
it not for the opposition of a con-

00:05:59.521 --> 00:06:05.540
trary force which restrains them to, and detains
them in their orbits, which

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:11.680
I therefore call centripetal, would fly off
in right lines, with an uniform

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:18.430
motion. A projectile, if it was not for the
force of gravity, would not deviate

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:23.860
towards the earth, but would go off from it
in a right line, and that with an

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:30.290
uniform motion, if the resistance of the air
was taken away. It is by its grav-

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:36.180
ity that it is drawn aside continually from
its rectilinear course, and made to

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:42.330
deviate towards the earth, more or less, according
to the force of its gravity,

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:48.650
and the velocity of its motion. The less its
gravity is, or the quantity of its

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:54.660
matter, or the greater the velocity with which
it is projected, the less will it

00:06:54.660 --> 00:07:01.570
deviate from a rectilinear course, and the
farther it will go. If a leaden ball,

00:07:01.570 --> 00:07:06.300
projected from the top of a mountain by the
force of gunpowder, with a

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:12.620
given velocity, and in a direction parallel
to the horizon, is carried ina curved

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:18.560
line to the distance of two miles before it
falls to the ground; the same, if

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:23.290
the resistance of the air were taken away,
with a double or decuple velocity,

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:31.090
would fly twice or ten times as far. And by
increasing the velocity, we may at

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:35.960
pleasure increase the distance to which it
might be projected, and diminish

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:41.240
the curvature of the line which it might describe,
till at last it should fall at

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:49.090
the distance of 10, 30, or go degrees, or
even might go quite round the whole

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:55.870
earth before it falls; or lastly, so that
it might never fall to the earth, but go

00:07:55.870 --> 00:08:03.220
forwards into the celestial spaces, and proceed
in its motion 7 infinitum.

00:08:03.220 --> 00:08:07.811
And after the same manner that a projectile,
by the force of gravity, may

00:08:07.811 --> 00:08:14.240
be made to revolve in an orbit, and go round
the whole earth, the moon

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:19.060
also, either by the force of gravity, if it
is endued with gravity, or by any

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:24.400
other force, that impels it towards the earth,
may be continually drawn

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:30.190
aside towards the earth, out of the rectilinear
way which by its innate force

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:35.360
it would pursue; and would be made to revolve
in the orbit which it now

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:40.490
describes; nor could the moon without some
such force be retained in its

00:08:40.490 --> 00:08:47.420
orbit. If this force was too small, it would
not sufficiently turn the moon

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:52.940
out of a rectilinear course; if it was too
great, it would turn it too much,

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:58.089
and draw down the moon from its orbit towards
the earth. It is necessary

00:08:58.089 --> 00:09:03.380
that the force be of a just quantity, and
it belongs to the mathematicians to

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:09.210
find the force that may serve exactly to retain
a body in a given orbit with

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:15.430
a given velocity; and vice versa, to determine
the curvilinear way into which

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:21.240
a body projected from a given place, with
a given velocity, may be made to

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:27.490
deviate from its natural rectilinear way,
by means of a given force.

00:09:27.490 --> 00:09:32.670
The quantity of any centripetal force may
be considered as of three

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:40.750
kinds: absolute, accelerative, and motive.

00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:45.630
DEFINITION VI
The absolute quantity of a centripetal force

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:50.890
is the measure of the same,
proportional to the efficacy of the cause

00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:56.520
that propagates it from the centre,
through the spaces round about.

00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:03.530
Thus the magnetic force is greater in one
loadstone and less in another,

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:07.210
according to their sizes and strength of intensity.

00:10:07.210 --> 00:10:13.130
DEFINITION VII
The accelerative quantity of a centripetal

00:10:13.130 --> 00:10:19.420
force is the measure of the same,
proportional to the velocity which it generates

00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:21.110
in a given time.

00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:29.280
Thus the force of the same loadstone is greater
at a less distance, and less

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:36.480
at a greater: also the force of gravity is
greater in valleys, less on tops of

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:42.451
exceeding high mountains; and yet less (as
shall hereafter be shown), at

00:10:42.451 --> 00:10:48.930
greater distances from the body of the earth;
but at equal distances, it is the

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:54.200
same everywhere; because (taking away, or
allowing for, the resistance of

00:10:54.200 --> 00:11:02.240
the air), it equally accelerates all falling
bodies, whether heavy or light,

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:04.670
great or small.

00:11:04.670 --> 00:11:09.590
DEFINITION VIII
The motive quantity of acentripetal force

00:11:09.590 --> 00:11:14.400
is the measure of the same, pro-
portional to the motion which it generates

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:16.150
in a given time.

00:11:16.150 --> 00:11:23.270
Thus the weight is greater in a greater body,
less in a less body; and, in

00:11:23.270 --> 00:11:30.420
the same body, it is greater near to the earth,
and less at remoter distances.

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:36.250
This sort of quantity is the centripetency,
or propension of the whole body

00:11:36.250 --> 00:11:42.980
towards the centre, or, as I may say, its
weight; and it is always known by

00:11:42.980 --> 00:11:48.279
the quantity of an equal and contrary force
just sufficient to hinder the

00:11:48.279 --> 00:11:51.710
descent of the body.

00:11:51.710 --> 00:11:58.881
These quantities of forces, we may, for the
sake of brevity, call by the

00:11:58.881 --> 00:12:06.510
names of motive, accelerative, and absolute
forces; and, for the sake of distinction,

00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:10.770
consider them with respect to the bodies that
tend to the centre,

00:12:10.770 --> 00:12:16.080
to the places of those bodies, and to the
centre of force towards which they

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:22.610
tend; that is to say, I refer the motive force
to the body as an endeavor and

00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:28.310
propensity of the whole towards a centre,
arising from the propensities of

00:12:28.310 --> 00:12:34.350
the several parts taken together; the accelerative
force to the place of the

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:40.080
body, as a certain power diffused from the
centre to all places around to

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:45.570
move the bodies that are in them; and the
absolute force to the centre, as

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:51.029
endued with some cause, without which those
motive forces would not be

00:12:51.029 --> 00:12:56.750
propagated through the spaces round about;
whether that cause be some

00:12:56.750 --> 00:13:03.230
central body (such as is the magnet in the
centre of the magnetic force, or

00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:07.810
the earth in the centre of the gravitating
force), or anything else that does

00:13:07.810 --> 00:13:13.950
not yet appear. For I here design only to
give a mathematical notion of

00:13:13.950 --> 00:13:20.400
those forces, without considering their physical
causes and seats.

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:26.970
Wherefore the accelerative force will stand
in the same relation to the

00:13:26.970 --> 00:13:33.510
motive, as celerity does to motion. For the
quantity of motion arises from

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:39.160
the celerity multiplied by the quantity of
matter; and the motive force

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:44.940
arises from the accelerative force multiplied
by the same quantity of matter.

00:13:44.940 --> 00:13:52.920
For the sum of the actions of the accelerative
force, upon the several particles of the body,

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:58.000
is the motive force of the whole. Hence it
is, that near the

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:03.791
surface of the earth, where the accelerative
gravity, or force productive of

00:14:03.791 --> 00:14:11.040
gravity, in all bodies is the same, the motive
gravity o. the weight is as the

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:16.310
body; but if we should ascend to higher regions,
where the accelerative

00:14:16.310 --> 00:14:22.210
gravity is less, the weight would be equally
diminished, and would always

00:14:22.210 --> 00:14:28.430
be as the product of the body, by the accelerative
gravity. So in those re-

00:14:28.430 --> 00:14:34.149
gions, where the accelerative gravity is diminished
into one-half, the weight

00:14:34.149 --> 00:14:41.820
of a body two or three times less, will be
four or six times less.

00:14:41.820 --> 00:14:48.420
I likewise call attractions and impulses,
in the same sense, accelerative,

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:54.390
and motive; and use the words attraction,
impulse, or propensity of any

00:14:54.390 --> 00:15:00.980
sort towards a centre, promiscuously, and
indifferently, one for another;

00:15:00.980 --> 00:15:06.120
considering those forces not physically, but
mathematically: wherefore the

00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:13.250
reader is not to imagine that by those words
I anywhere take upon me to

00:15:13.250 --> 00:15:18.350
define the kind, or the manner of any action,
the causes or the physical

00:15:18.350 --> 00:15:24.410
reason thereof, or that I attribute forces,
in a true and physical sense, to

00:15:24.410 --> 00:15:30.020
certain centres (which are only mathematical
points); when at any time I

00:15:30.020 --> 00:15:39.680
happen to speak of centres as attracting,
or as endued with attractive powers.

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:44.740
SCHOLIUM

00:15:44.740 --> 00:15:54.580
Hitherto I have laid down the definitions
of such words as are less

00:15:54.580 --> 00:15:59.500
known, and explained the sense in which I
would have them to be under-

00:15:59.500 --> 00:16:05.630
stood in the following discourse. I do not
define time, space, place, and

00:16:05.630 --> 00:16:11.560
motion, as being well known to all. Only I
must observe, that the common

00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:18.089
people conceive those quantities under no
other notions but from the relation they bear

00:16:18.089 --> 00:16:23.340
to sensible objects. And thence arise certain
prejudices, for

00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:29.740
the removing of which it will be convenient
to distinguish them into absolute and relative,

00:16:29.740 --> 00:16:33.460
true and apparent, mathematical and common.

00:16:33.460 --> 00:16:41.600
I. Absolute, true, and mathematical time,
of itself} and from its own

00:16:41.600 --> 00:16:47.480
nature, flows equably without relation to
anything external, and by another

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:56.089
name is called duration: relative, apparent,
and common time, is some sensible and external

00:16:56.089 --> 00:17:00.800
(whether accurate or unequable) measure of
duration by

00:17:00.800 --> 00:17:06.569
the means of motion, which is commonly used
instead of true time; such

00:17:06.569 --> 00:17:12.400
as an hour, a day, a month, a year.

00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:19.050
II. Absolute space, in its own nature, without
relation to anything exter-

00:17:19.050 --> 00:17:25.289
nal, remains always similar and immovable.
Relative space is some movable

00:17:25.289 --> 00:17:30.500
dimension or measure of the absolute spaces;
which our senses determine

00:17:30.500 --> 00:17:35.320
by its position to bodies; and which is commonly
taken for immovable

00:17:35.320 --> 00:17:42.760
space; such is the dimension of a subterraneous,
an aerial, or celestial space,

00:17:42.760 --> 00:17:47.730
determined by its position in respect of the
earth. Absolute and relative

00:17:47.730 --> 00:17:52.980
space are the same in figure and magnitude;
but they do not remain always

00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:59.529
numerically the same. For if the earth, for
instance, moves, a space of our

00:17:59.529 --> 00:18:05.520
air, which relatively and in respect of the
earth remains always the same,

00:18:05.520 --> 00:18:11.289
will at one time be one part of the absolute
space into which the air passes;

00:18:11.289 --> 00:18:16.889
at another time it will be another part of
the same, and so, absolutely under-

00:18:16.889 --> 00:18:21.649
stood, it will be continually changed.

00:18:21.649 --> 00:18:29.529
III. Place is a part of space which a body
takes up, and is according to the

00:18:29.529 --> 00:18:37.620
space, either absolute or relative. I say,
a part of space; not the situation, nor

00:18:37.620 --> 00:18:43.690
the external surface of the body. For the
places of equal solids are always

00:18:43.690 --> 00:18:49.620
equal; but their surfaces, by reason of their
dissimilar figures, are often

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:56.179
unequal. Positions properly have no quantity,
nor are they so much the

00:18:56.179 --> 00:19:01.869
places themselves, as the properties of places.
The motion of the whole is

00:19:01.869 --> 00:19:07.351
the same with the sum of the motions of the
parts; that is, the translation

00:19:07.351 --> 00:19:13.489
of the whole, out of its place, is the same
thing with the sum of the translations of

00:19:13.489 --> 00:19:15.970
the parts out of their places

00:19:15.970 --> 00:19:22.119
Absolute time, in astronomy, is distinguished
from relative, by the equation or correction

00:19:22.119 --> 00:19:28.230
of the apparent time. For the natural days
are truly un-equal, though they are commonly

00:19:28.230 --> 00:19:34.649
considered as equal, and used for a measure
of time; astronomers correct this inequality

00:19:34.649 --> 00:19:39.639
that they may measure the
celestial motions by a more accurate time.

00:19:39.639 --> 00:19:46.239
It may be, that there is no such
thing as an equable motion, whereby time may

00:19:46.239 --> 00:19:51.740
be accurately measured.
All motions may be accelerated and retarded,

00:19:51.740 --> 00:19:58.710
but the flowing of absolute
time is not liable to any change. The duration

00:19:58.710 --> 00:20:04.779
or perseverance of the existence of things
remains the same, whether the motions are

00:20:04.779 --> 00:20:09.730
swift or slow, or
none at all: and therefore this duration ought

00:20:09.730 --> 00:20:15.039
to be distinguished from what
are only sensible measures thereof; and from

00:20:15.039 --> 00:20:20.529
which we deduce it, by means
of the astronomical equation. The necessity

00:20:20.529 --> 00:20:27.549
of this equation, for determining the times
of a phenomenon, is evinced as well from the

00:20:27.549 --> 00:20:31.369
experiments
of the pendulum clock, as by eclipses of the

00:20:31.369 --> 00:20:35.299
satellites of Jupiter.

00:20:35.299 --> 00:20:42.289
As the order of the parts of time is immutable,
so also is the order of the

00:20:42.289 --> 00:20:48.860
parts of space. Suppose those parts to be
moved out of their places, and

00:20:48.860 --> 00:20:54.790
they will be moved (if the expression may
be allowed) out of themselves.

00:20:54.790 --> 00:21:02.350
For times and spaces are, as it were, the
places as well of themselves as of

00:21:02.350 --> 00:21:08.950
all other things. All things are placed in
time as to order of succession; and

00:21:08.950 --> 00:21:15.360
in space as to order of situation. It is from
their essence or nature that they

00:21:15.360 --> 00:21:20.850
are places; and that the primary places of
things should be movable, is

00:21:20.850 --> 00:21:26.619
absurd. These are therefore the absolute places;
and translations out of

00:21:26.619 --> 00:21:30.980
those places, are the only absolute motions.

00:21:30.980 --> 00:21:37.820
But because the parts of space cannot be seen,
or distinguished from one

00:21:37.820 --> 00:21:43.740
another by our senses, therefore in their
stead we use sensible measures of

00:21:43.740 --> 00:21:50.759
them. For from the positions and distances
of things from any body considered as immovable,

00:21:50.759 --> 00:21:54.419
we define all places; and then with respect
to such

00:21:54.419 --> 00:22:00.259
places, we estimate all motions, considering
bodies as transferred from some

00:22:00.259 --> 00:22:07.450
of those places into others. And so, instead
of absolute places and motions,

00:22:07.450 --> 00:22:12.119
we use relative ones; and that without any
inconvenience in common

00:22:12.119 --> 00:22:17.519
affairs; but in philosophical disquisitions,
we ought to abstract from our

00:22:17.519 --> 00:22:23.779
senses, and consider things themselves, distinct
from what are only sensible

00:22:23.779 --> 00:22:29.519
measures of them. For it may be that there
is no body really at rest, to which

00:22:29.519 --> 00:22:33.220
the places and motions of others may be referred.

00:22:33.220 --> 00:22:39.350
But we may distinguish rest and motion, absolute
and relative, one from

00:22:39.350 --> 00:22:45.480
the other by their properties, causes, and
effects. It is a property of rest, that

00:22:45.480 --> 00:22:52.509
bodies really at rest do rest in respect to
one another. And therefore as it is

00:22:52.509 --> 00:22:58.899
possible, that in the remote regions of the
fixed stars, or perhaps far beyond

00:22:58.899 --> 00:23:04.879
them, there may be some body absolutely at
rest; but impossible to know,

00:23:04.879 --> 00:23:09.590
from the position of bodies to one another
in our regions, whether any of

00:23:09.590 --> 00:23:15.269
these do keep the same position to that remote
body, it follows that absolute

00:23:15.269 --> 00:23:21.029
rest cannot be determined from the position
of bodies in our regions.

00:23:21.029 --> 00:23:26.989
It is a property of motion, that the parts,
which retain given positions to

00:23:26.989 --> 00:23:33.549
their wholes, do partake of the motions of
those wholes. For all the parts

00:23:33.549 --> 00:23:38.309
of revolving bodies endeavor to recede from
the axis of motion; and the

00:23:38.309 --> 00:23:43.880
impetus of bodies moving forwards arises from
the joint impetus of all the

00:23:43.880 --> 00:23:49.929
parts. Therefore, if surrounding bodies are
moved, those that are relatively

00:23:49.929 --> 00:23:55.799
at rest within them will partake of their
motion. Upon which account, the

00:23:55.799 --> 00:24:00.700
true and absolute motion of a body cannot
be determined by the translation

00:24:00.700 --> 00:24:07.080
of it from those which only seem to rest;
for the external bodies ought not

00:24:07.080 --> 00:24:14.120
only to appear at rest, but to be really at
rest. For otherwise, all included

00:24:14.120 --> 00:24:18.990
bodies, besides their translation from near
the surrounding ones, partake

00:24:18.990 --> 00:24:24.179
likewise of their true motions; and though
that translation were not made,

00:24:24.179 --> 00:24:30.590
they would not be really at rest, but only
seem to be so. For the surrounding

00:24:30.590 --> 00:24:36.910
bodies stand in the like relation to the surrounded
as the exterior part of a

00:24:36.910 --> 00:24:43.860
whole does to the interior, or as the shell
does to the kernel; but if the shell

00:24:43.860 --> 00:24:48.840
moves, the kernel will also move, as being
part of the whole, without any

00:24:48.840 --> 00:24:51.489
removal from near the shell.

00:24:51.489 --> 00:24:59.629
A property, near akin to the preceding, is
this, that if a place is moved,

00:24:59.629 --> 00:25:04.889
whatever is placed therein moves along with
it; and therefore a body, which

00:25:04.889 --> 00:25:10.850
is moved from a place in motion, partakes
also of the motion of its place.

00:25:10.850 --> 00:25:15.779
Upon which account, all motions, from places
in motion, are no other than

00:25:15.779 --> 00:25:22.529
parts of entire and absolute motions; and
every entire motion is composed

00:25:22.529 --> 00:25:28.070
of the motion of the body out of its first
place, and the motion of this place

00:25:28.070 --> 00:25:33.320
out of its place; and so on, until we come
to some immovable place, as in

00:25:33.320 --> 00:25:36.530
the before-mentioned example of the sailor.
Wherefore, entire and absolute motions can

00:25:36.530 --> 00:25:41.129
be no otherwise determined than by immovable
places;

00:25:41.129 --> 00:25:46.679
and for that reason I did before refer those
absolute motions to immovable

00:25:46.679 --> 00:25:54.649
places, but relative ones to movable places.
Now no other places are immovable but those

00:25:54.649 --> 00:25:59.359
that, from infinity to infinity, do all retain
the same given

00:25:59.359 --> 00:26:06.320
position one to another ; and upon this account
must ever remain unmoved;

00:26:06.320 --> 00:26:13.489
and do thereby constitute immovable space.

00:26:13.489 --> 00:26:18.269
The causes by which true and relative motions
are distinguished, one

00:26:18.269 --> 00:26:24.330
from the other, are the forces impressed upon
bodies to generate motion.

00:26:24.330 --> 00:26:29.690
True motion is neither generated nor altered,
but by some force impressed

00:26:29.690 --> 00:26:35.239
upon the body moved; but relative motion may
be generated or altered

00:26:35.239 --> 00:26:40.049
without any force impressed upon the body.
For it is sufficient only to

00:26:40.049 --> 00:26:46.009
impress some force on other bodies with which
the former is compared, that

00:26:46.009 --> 00:26:51.479
by their giving way, that relation may be
changed, in which the relative

00:26:51.479 --> 00:26:58.440
rest or motion of this other body did consist.
Again, true motion suffers

00:26:58.440 --> 00:27:03.279
always some change from any force impressed
upon the moving body;

00:27:03.279 --> 00:27:09.809
_ but relative motion does not necessarily
undergo any change by such forces.

00:27:09.809 --> 00:27:14.880
For if the same forces are likewise impressed
on those other bodies, with

00:27:14.880 --> 00:27:19.609
which the comparison is made, that the relative
position may be preserved,

00:27:19.609 --> 00:27:26.529
then that condition will be preserved in which
the relative motion consists.

00:27:26.529 --> 00:27:31.370
And therefore any relative motion may be changed
when the true motion

00:27:31.370 --> 00:27:37.070
remains unaltered, and the relative may be
preserved when the true suffers

00:27:37.070 --> 00:27:52.609
some change. Thus, true motion by no means
consists in such relations.

00:27:52.609 --> 00:27:57.389
The effects which distinguish absolute from
relative motion are, the

00:27:57.389 --> 00:28:03.190
forces of receding from the axis of circular
motion. For there are no such

00:28:03.190 --> 00:28:09.149
forces in a circular motion purely relative,
but in a true and absolute circular

00:28:09.149 --> 00:28:14.379
motion, they are greater or less, according
to the quantity of the motion.

00:28:14.379 --> 00:28:21.049
If a vessel, hung by a long cord, is so often
turned about that the cord is

00:28:21.049 --> 00:28:26.279
strongly twisted, then filled with water,
and held at rest together with the

00:28:26.279 --> 00:28:32.389
water ; thereupon, by the sudden action of
another force, it is whirled about

00:28:32.389 --> 00:28:39.070
the contrary way, and while the cord is untwisting
itself, the vessel continues for some time

00:28:39.070 --> 00:28:44.330
in this motion; the surface of the water will
at first be

00:28:44.330 --> 00:28:51.019
plain, as before the vessel began to move;
but after that, the vessel, by gradually communicating

00:28:51.019 --> 00:28:54.690
its motion to the water, will make it begin
sensibly

00:28:54.690 --> 00:29:00.299
to revolve, and recede by little and little
from the middle, and ascend to the

00:29:00.299 --> 00:29:07.700
sides of the vessel, forming itself into a
concave figure (as I have experienced), and

00:29:07.700 --> 00:29:11.940
the swifter the motion becomes, the higher
will the water rise,

00:29:11.940 --> 00:29:17.740
till at last, performing its revolutions in
the same times with the vessel, it

00:29:17.740 --> 00:29:23.989
becomes relatively at rest in it. This ascent
of the water shows its endeavor

00:29:23.989 --> 00:29:29.139
to recede from the axis of its motion; and
the true and absolute circular

00:29:29.139 --> 00:29:35.309
motion of the water, which is here directly
contrary to the relative, becomes

00:29:35.309 --> 00:29:41.590
known, and may be measured by this endeavor.
At first, when the relative

00:29:41.590 --> 00:29:46.120
motion of the water in the vessel was greatest,
it produced no endeavor to

00:29:46.120 --> 00:29:52.610
recede from the axis; the water showed no
tendency to the circumference,

00:29:52.610 --> 00:29:59.739
nor any ascent towards the sides of the vessel,
but remained of a plain surface, and therefore

00:29:59.739 --> 00:30:04.009
its true circular motion had not yet begun.
But after-

00:30:04.009 --> 00:30:09.479
wards, when the relative motion of the water
had decreased, the ascent

00:30:09.479 --> 00:30:14.059
thereof towards the sides of the vessel proved
its endeavor to recede from

00:30:14.059 --> 00:30:19.379
the axis; and this endeavor showed the real
circular motion of the water

00:30:19.379 --> 00:30:25.059
continually increasing, till it had acquired
its greatest quantity, when the

00:30:25.059 --> 00:30:30.500
water rested relatively in the vessel. And
therefore this endeavor does not

00:30:30.500 --> 00:30:36.389
depend upon any translation of the water in
respect of the ambient bodies,

00:30:36.389 --> 00:30:41.830
nor can true circular motion be defined by
such translation. There is only

00:30:41.830 --> 00:30:47.289
one real circular motion of any one revolving
body, corresponding to only

00:30:47.289 --> 00:30:52.999
one power of endeavoring to recede from its
axis of motion, as its proper

00:30:52.999 --> 00:30:59.309
and adequate effect; but relative motions,
in one and the same body, are

00:30:59.309 --> 00:31:03.789
innumerable, according to the various relations
it bears to external bodies,

00:31:03.789 --> 00:31:10.399
and, like other relations, are altogether
destitute of any real effect, any other-

00:31:10.399 --> 00:31:17.519
wise than they may perhaps partake of that
one only true motion. And

00:31:17.519 --> 00:31:22.989
therefore in their system who suppose that
our heavens, revolving below

00:31:22.989 --> 00:31:28.500
the sphere of the fixed stars, carry the planets
along with them; the several

00:31:28.500 --> 00:31:33.080
parts of those heavens, and the planets, which
are indeed relatively at rest

00:31:33.080 --> 00:31:39.359
in their heavens, do yet really move. For
they change their position one to

00:31:39.359 --> 00:31:44.809
another (which never happens to bodies truly
at rest), and being carried

00:31:44.809 --> 00:31:51.460
together with their heavens, partake of their
motions, and as parts of revolving wholes,

00:31:51.460 --> 00:31:57.139
endeavor to recede from the axis of their
motions.

00:31:57.139 --> 00:32:01.019
Wherefore relative quantities are not the
quantities themselves, whose

00:32:01.019 --> 00:32:06.110
names they bear, but those sensible measures
of them (either accurate or

00:32:06.110 --> 00:32:10.249
inaccurate), which are commonly used instead
of the measured quantities

00:32:10.249 --> 00:32:16.909
themselves. And if the meaning of words is
to be determined by their use,

00:32:16.909 --> 00:32:22.729
then by the names time, space, place, and
motion, their [sensible] measures

00:32:22.729 --> 00:32:27.690
are properly to be understood; and the expression
will be unusual, and

00:32:27.690 --> 00:32:32.940
purely mathematical, if the measured quantities
themselves are meant. On

00:32:32.940 --> 00:32:37.649
this account, those violate the accuracy of
language, which ought to be kept

00:32:37.649 --> 00:32:43.320
precise, who interpret these words for the
measured quantities. Nor do those

00:32:43.320 --> 00:32:49.260
less defile the purity of mathematical and
philosophical truths, who con-

00:32:49.260 --> 00:32:58.909
found real quantities with their relations
and sensible measures.

00:32:58.909 --> 00:33:05.279
It is indeed a matter of great difficulty
to discover, and effectually to distinguish,

00:33:05.279 --> 00:33:09.460
the true motions of particular bodies from
the apparent; because

00:33:09.460 --> 00:33:14.879
the parts of that immovable space, in which
those motions are performed,

00:33:14.879 --> 00:33:20.370
do by no means come under the observation
of our senses. Yet the thing is

00:33:20.370 --> 00:33:26.070
not altogether desperate; for we have some
arguments to guide us, partly

00:33:26.070 --> 00:33:31.340
from the apparent motions, which are the differences
of the true motions;

00:33:31.340 --> 00:33:36.859
partly from the forces, which are the causes
and effects of the true motions.

00:33:36.859 --> 00:33:42.059
For instance, if two globes, kept at a given
distance one from the other by

00:33:42.059 --> 00:33:46.640
means of a cord that connects them, were revolved
about their common

00:33:46.640 --> 00:33:52.159
centre of gravity, we might, from the tension
of the cord, discover the

00:33:52.159 --> 00:33:57.179
endeavor of the globes to recede from the
axis of their motion, and from

00:33:57.179 --> 00:34:03.570
thence we might compute the quantity of their
circular motions. And then

00:34:03.570 --> 00:34:09.950
if any equal forces should be impressed at
once on the alternate faces of the

00:34:09.950 --> 00:34:15.379
globes to augment or diminish their circular
motions, from the increase or

00:34:15.379 --> 00:34:22.169
decrease of the tension of the cord, we might
infer the increment or decrement of their

00:34:22.169 --> 00:34:26.310
motions; and thence would be found on what
faces those

00:34:26.310 --> 00:34:32.149
forces ought to be impressed, that the motions
of the globes might be most

00:34:32.149 --> 00:34:38.020
augmented; that is, we might discover their
hindmost faces, or those which,

00:34:38.020 --> 00:34:44.919
in the circular motion, do follow. But the
faces which follow being known,

00:34:44.919 --> 00:34:50.069
and consequently the opposite ones that precede,
we should likewise know

00:34:50.069 --> 00:34:57.480
the determination of their motions. And thus
we might find both the quantity and the determination

00:34:57.480 --> 00:35:04.010
of this circular motion, even in an immense
vacuum, where there was nothing external or

00:35:04.010 --> 00:35:09.481
sensible with which the
globes could be compared. But now, if in that

00:35:09.481 --> 00:35:14.441
space some remote bodies
were placed that kept always a given position

00:35:14.441 --> 00:35:21.170
one to another, as the fixed
stars do in our regions, we could not indeed

00:35:21.170 --> 00:35:25.410
determine from the relative
translation of the globes among those bodies,

00:35:25.410 --> 00:35:30.049
whether the motion did
belong to the globes or to the bodies. But

00:35:30.049 --> 00:35:36.190
if we observed the cord, and found
that its tension was that very tension which

00:35:36.190 --> 00:35:42.710
the motions of the globes required, we might
conclude the motion to be in the globes, and

00:35:42.710 --> 00:35:48.130
the bodies
to be at rest; and then, lastly, from the

00:35:48.130 --> 00:35:52.710
translation of the globes among the
bodies, we should find the determination of

00:35:52.710 --> 00:35:59.349
their motions. But how we are
to obtain the true motions from their causes,

00:35:59.349 --> 00:36:05.500
effects, and apparent differences, and the
converse, shall be explained more at large

00:36:05.500 --> 00:36:10.809
in the following
treatise. For to this end it was that I composed

00:36:10.809 --> 00:36:12.809
it.

00:36:12.809 --> 00:36:21.400
AXIOMS, OR
LAWS OF MOTION

00:36:21.400 --> 00:36:31.750
LAW I
Every body continues in its state of rest,

00:36:31.750 --> 00:36:36.880
or of uniform motion in a right
line, unless it is compelled to change that

00:36:36.880 --> 00:36:43.980
state by forces impressed upon it.
PROJECTILES continue in their motions, so

00:36:43.980 --> 00:36:48.779
far as they are not retarded
Pp by the resistance of the air, or impelled

00:36:48.779 --> 00:36:54.160
downwards by the force of
gravity. A top, whose parts by their cohesion

00:36:54.160 --> 00:36:59.760
are continually drawn
aside from rectilinear motions, does not cease

00:36:59.760 --> 00:37:06.279
its rotation, otherwise than
as it is retarded by the air. The greater

00:37:06.279 --> 00:37:11.630
bodies of the planets and comets,
meeting with less resistance in freer spaces,

00:37:11.630 --> 00:37:16.510
preserve their motions both
progressive and circular for a much longer

00:37:16.510 --> 00:37:20.450
time.
LAW II

00:37:20.450 --> 00:37:26.609
The change of motion is proportional to the
motive force impressed; and

00:37:26.609 --> 00:37:32.930
1s made in the direction of the right line
in which that force is impressed.

00:37:32.930 --> 00:37:38.770
If any force generates a motion, a double
force will generate double the

00:37:38.770 --> 00:37:44.029
motion, a triple force triple the motion,
whether that force be impressed

00:37:44.029 --> 00:37:49.930
altogether and at once, or gradually and successively.
And this motion

00:37:49.930 --> 00:37:56.130
(being always directed the same way with the
generating force), if the

00:37:56.130 --> 00:38:01.520
body moved before, is added to or subtracted
from the former motion,

00:38:01.520 --> 00:38:07.940
according as they directly conspire with or
are directly contrary to each

00:38:07.940 --> 00:38:14.339
other; or obliquely joined, when they are
oblique, so as to produce a new

00:38:14.339 --> 00:38:17.940
motion compounded from the determination of
both.

00:38:17.940 --> 00:38:20.730
LAW III

00:38:20.730 --> 00:38:26.819
To every action there is always opposed an
equal reaction: or, the mutual

00:38:26.819 --> 00:38:32.430
actions of two bodies upon each other are
always equal, and directed to

00:38:32.430 --> 00:38:34.190
contrary parts.

00:38:34.190 --> 00:38:41.089
Whatever draws or presses another is as much
drawn or pressed by that

00:38:41.089 --> 00:38:47.380
other. If you press a stone with your finger,
the finger is also pressed by the

00:38:47.380 --> 00:38:54.880
stone. If a horse draws a stone tied to a
rope, the horse (if I may so say) will

00:38:54.880 --> 00:39:00.980
be equally drawn back towards the stone; for
the distended rope, by the

00:39:00.980 --> 00:39:06.039
same endeavor to relax or unbend itself, will
draw the horse as much

00:39:06.039 --> 00:39:11.359
towards the stone as it does the stone towards
the horse, and will obstruct

00:39:11.359 --> 00:39:17.440
the progress of the one as much as it advances
that of the other. If a body

00:39:17.440 --> 00:39:23.779
impinge upon another, and by its force change
the motion of the other, that

00:39:23.779 --> 00:39:29.250
body also (because of the equality of the
mutual pressure) will undergo an

00:39:29.250 --> 00:39:35.400
equal change, in its own motion, towards the
contrary part. The changes

00:39:35.400 --> 00:39:40.839
made by these actions are equal, not in the
velocities but in the motions of

00:39:40.839 --> 00:39:46.339
bodies; that is to say, if the bodies are
not hindered by any other impedi-

00:39:46.339 --> 00:39:51.630
ments. For, because the motions are equally
changed, the changes of the

00:39:51.630 --> 00:39:56.520
velocities made towards contrary parts are
inversely proportional to the

00:39:56.520 --> 00:40:05.089
bodies. This law takes place also in attractions,
as will be proved in the next

00:40:05.089 --> 00:40:06.089
Scholium.

00:40:06.089 --> 00:40:11.490
COROLLARY I
A body, acted on by two forces simultaneously,

00:40:11.490 --> 00:40:17.490
will describe the diagonal
of a parallelogram in the same time as it

00:40:17.490 --> 00:40:22.849
would describe the sides by those
forces separately.

00:40:22.849 --> 00:40:28.109
COROLLARY II
And hence is explained the composition of

00:40:28.109 --> 00:40:38.280
any one direct force AD, out of
any two oblique forces AC and CD; and, on

00:40:38.280 --> 00:40:46.740
the contrary, the resolution of
any one direct force AD into two oblique forces

00:40:46.740 --> 00:41:01.180
AC and CD: which composition and resolution
are abundantly confirmed from mechanics.

00:41:01.180 --> 00:41:02.930
COROLLARY III

00:41:02.930 --> 00:41:07.069
The quantity of motion, which is obtained
by taking the sum of the motions

00:41:07.069 --> 00:41:12.480
directed towards the same parts, and the difference
of those that are directed

00:41:12.480 --> 00:41:23.490
to contrary parts, suffers no change from
the action of bodies among themselves.

00:41:23.490 --> 00:41:28.269
COROLLARY IV
The common centre of gravity of two or more

00:41:28.269 --> 00:41:33.490
bodies does not alter its state
of motion or rest by the actions of the bodies

00:41:33.490 --> 00:41:38.970
among themselves; and there-
fore the common centre of gravity of all bodies

00:41:38.970 --> 00:41:43.460
acting upon each other
(excluding external actions and impediments)

00:41:43.460 --> 00:41:50.340
is either at rest, or moves
uniformly in a right line.

00:41:50.340 --> 00:41:56.070
COROLLARY V

00:41:56.070 --> 00:42:01.799
The motions of bodies included in a given
space are the same among them-

00:42:01.799 --> 00:42:07.160
selves, whether that space 1s at rest, or
moves uniformly forwards in aright

00:42:07.160 --> 00:42:11.050
line without any circular motion.

00:42:11.050 --> 00:42:18.539
COROLLARY VI
If bodies, moved in any manner among themselves,

00:42:18.539 --> 00:42:25.859
are urged in the direction of parallel lines
by equal accelerative forces, they will all

00:42:25.859 --> 00:42:29.900
continue to
move among themselves, after the same manner

00:42:29.900 --> 00:42:34.890
as if they had not been
urged by those forces.

00:42:34.890 --> 00:42:37.619
SCHOLIUM

00:42:37.619 --> 00:42:44.191
Hitherto I have laid down such principles
as have been received by mathematicians, and

00:42:44.191 --> 00:42:48.710
are confirmed by abundance of experiments.
By the first

00:42:48.710 --> 00:42:54.240
two Laws and the first two Corollaries, Galileo
discovered that the descent

00:42:54.240 --> 00:43:01.589
of bodies varied as the square of the time
and that the motion of projectiles was in

00:43:01.589 --> 00:43:08.470
the curve of a parabola; experience
agreeing with both, unless so far as these

00:43:08.470 --> 00:43:15.000
motions are a little retarded by the
resistance of the air. When a body is faliing,

00:43:15.000 --> 00:43:21.350
the uniform force of its gravity
acting equally, impresses, in equal intervals

00:43:21.350 --> 00:43:28.339
of time, equal forces upon that
body, and therefore generates equal velocities;

00:43:28.339 --> 00:43:33.039
and in the whole time im-
presses a whole force, and generates a whole

00:43:33.039 --> 00:43:39.960
velocity proportional to the
time. And the spaces described in proportional

00:43:39.960 --> 00:43:46.069
times are as the product of
the velocities and the times; that is, as

00:43:46.069 --> 00:43:52.280
the squares of the times. And when
a body is thrown upwards, its uniform gravity

00:43:52.280 --> 00:43:58.619
impresses forces and reduces
velocities proportional to the times; and

00:43:58.619 --> 00:44:04.490
the times of ascending to the great-
est heights are as the velocities to be taken

00:44:04.490 --> 00:44:12.279
away, and those heights are as the
product of the velocities and the times, or

00:44:12.279 --> 00:44:18.579
as the squares of the velocities.
And if a body be projected in any direction,

00:44:18.579 --> 00:44:24.210
the motion arising from its
projection is compounded with the motion arising

00:44:24.210 --> 00:44:33.599
from its gravity. Thus,

00:44:33.599 --> 00:44:44.049
if the body A by its motion of projection
alone could describe in a given

00:44:44.049 --> 00:44:51.519
time the right line AB, and with its motion
of falling alone could describe

00:44:51.519 --> 00:45:00.059
in the same time the altitude AC; complete
the parallelogram ABCD, and

00:45:00.059 --> 00:45:05.480
the body by that compounded motion will at
the end of the time be found

00:45:05.480 --> 00:45:14.390
in the place D; and the curved line AED, which
B that body describes, will be a parabola,

00:45:14.390 --> 00:45:19.670
to which the right line AB will be a tangent
at A; and whose

00:45:19.670 --> 00:45:26.359
ordinate BD will be as the square of the line
AB.

00:45:26.359 --> 00:45:32.740
On the same Laws and Corollaries depend those
things which have been demonstrated concerning

00:45:32.740 --> 00:45:38.789
the times of the vibration of pendulums, and
are confirmed by the daily experiments of

00:45:38.789 --> 00:45:43.340
pendulum clocks. By the same, together with
Law III, Sir

00:45:43.340 --> 00:45:51.569
Christopher Wren, Dr. Wallis, and Mr. Huygens,
the greatest geometers of our times, did severally

00:45:51.569 --> 00:45:56.700
determine the rules of
the impact and reflection of hard bodies,

00:45:56.700 --> 00:46:02.680
and about the same time communicated their
discoveries to the Royal Society, exactly

00:46:02.680 --> 00:46:07.430
agreeing among
themselves as to those rules. Dr. Wallis,

00:46:07.430 --> 00:46:12.660
indeed, was somewhat earlier in
the publication; then followed Sir Christopher

00:46:12.660 --> 00:46:20.210
Wren, and, lastly, Mr. Huygens. But Sir Christopher
Wren confirmed the truth of the thing before

00:46:20.210 --> 00:46:26.299
the Royal Society by the experiments on pendulums,
which M. Mariotte

00:46:26.299 --> 00:46:32.930
soon after thought fit to explain in a treatise
entirely upon that subject.

00:46:32.930 --> 00:46:38.350
But to bring this experiment to an accurate
agreement with the theory, we

00:46:38.350 --> 00:46:43.849
are to have due regard as well to the resistance
of the air as to the elastic

00:46:43.849 --> 00:46:51.089
force of the concurring bodies.
Let the spherical bodies A, B be

00:46:51.089 --> 00:46:58.650
suspended by the parallel and
equal strings AC, BD, from the

00:46:58.650 --> 00:47:06.920
centres C, D. About these centres, with those
lengths as radii,

00:47:06.920 --> 00:47:14.750
describe the semicircles EAF, on
GBH, bisected respectively by

00:47:14.750 --> 00:47:25.840
the radii CA, DB. Bring the
body A to any point R of the arc EAF, and

00:47:25.840 --> 00:47:32.380
(withdrawing the body B) let
it go from thence, and after one oscillation

00:47:32.380 --> 00:47:34.830
suppose it to return to the point

00:47:34.830 --> 00:47:43.289
V: then RV will be the retardation arising
from the resistance of the air.

00:47:43.289 --> 00:47:55.000
Of this RV let ST be a fourth part, situated
in the middle, namely, so that

00:47:55.000 --> 00:47:57.900
RS=TV,
and

00:47:57.900 --> 00:48:02.240
RS : ST =3:2,

00:48:02.240 --> 00:48:09.369
then will ST represent very nearly the retardation
during the descent from

00:48:09.369 --> 00:48:17.349
S to A. Restore the body B to its place: and,
supposing the body A to be let

00:48:17.349 --> 00:48:24.890
fall from the point S, the velocity thereof
in the place of reflection A, with-

00:48:24.890 --> 00:48:30.980
out sensible error, will be the same as if
it had descended in vacuo from the

00:48:30.980 --> 00:48:37.800
point T. Upon which account this velocity
may be represented by the chord

00:48:37.800 --> 00:48:46.059
of the arc TA. For it is a proposition well
known to geometers, that the

00:48:46.059 --> 00:48:51.880
velocity of a pendulous body in the lowest
point is as the chord of the arc

00:48:51.880 --> 00:48:59.319
which it has described in its descent. After
reflection, suppose the body A

00:48:59.319 --> 00:49:07.530
comes to the place s, and the body B to the
place &amp;. Withdraw the body B,

00:49:07.530 --> 00:49:13.720
and find the place v, from which if the body
A, being let go, should after

00:49:13.720 --> 00:49:22.920
one oscillation return to the place r, st
may be a fourth part of rv, so placed

00:49:22.920 --> 00:49:30.090
in the middle thereof as to leave rs equal
to tv, and let the chord of the arc

00:49:30.090 --> 00:49:37.259
tA represent the velocity which the body A
had in the place A immediately

00:49:37.259 --> 00:49:44.839
after reflection. For t will be the true and
correct place to which the body A

00:49:44.839 --> 00:49:50.930
should have ascended, if the resistance of
the air had been taken off. In the

00:49:50.930 --> 00:49:57.460
same way we are to correct the place &amp; to
which the body B ascends, by find-

00:49:57.460 --> 00:50:04.210
ing the place / to which it should have ascended
im vacuo. And thus every-

00:50:04.210 --> 00:50:09.029
thing may be subjected to experiment, in the
same manner as if we were

00:50:09.029 --> 00:50:14.690
really placed 1m vacuo. These things being
done, we are to take the product

00:50:14.690 --> 00:50:23.529
(if I may so say) of the body A, by the chord
of the arc TA (which represents its velocity),

00:50:23.529 --> 00:50:27.569
that we may have its motion in the place A
immediately

00:50:27.569 --> 00:50:33.930
before reflection; and then by the chord of
the arc zA, that we may have its

00:50:33.930 --> 00:50:41.230
motion in the place A immediately after reflection.
And so we are to take

00:50:41.230 --> 00:50:49.349
the product of the body B by the chord of
the arc B/, that we may have the

00:50:49.349 --> 00:50:55.230
motion of the same immediately after reflection.
And in like manner, when

00:50:55.230 --> 00:51:00.680
two bodies are let go together from different
places, we are to find the

00:51:00.680 --> 00:51:06.529
motion of each, as well before as after reflection;
and then we may compare

00:51:06.529 --> 00:51:12.170
the motions between themselves, and collect
the effects of the reflection.

00:51:12.170 --> 00:51:17.750
Thus trying the thing with pendulums of ro
feet, in unequal as well as

00:51:17.750 --> 00:51:24.930
equal bodies, and making the bodies to concur
after a descent through large

00:51:24.930 --> 00:51:33.130
spaces, as of 8, 12, or 16 feet, I found always,
without an error of 3 inches,

00:51:33.130 --> 00:51:40.410
that when the bodies concurred together directly,
equal changes towards

00:51:40.410 --> 00:51:46.000
the contrary parts were produced in their
motions, and, of consequence,

00:51:46.000 --> 00:51:51.630
that the action and reaction were always equal.
As if the body A impinged

00:51:51.630 --> 00:51:58.529
upon the body B at rest with 9 parts of motion,
and losing 7, proceeded

00:51:58.529 --> 00:52:04.980
after reflection with 2, the body B was carried
backwards with those 7 parts.

00:52:04.980 --> 00:52:10.269
If the bodies concurred with contrary motions,
A with 12 parts of motion,

00:52:10.269 --> 00:52:18.279
and B with 6, then if A receded with 2, B
receded with 8; namely, with a

00:52:18.279 --> 00:52:26.250
deduction of 14 parts of motion on each side.
For from the motion of A

00:52:26.250 --> 00:52:32.490
subtracting 12 parts, nothing will remain;
but subtracting 2 parts more, a

00:52:32.490 --> 00:52:38.130
motion will be generated of 2 parts towards
the contrary way; and so, from

00:52:38.130 --> 00:52:44.800
the motion of the body B of 6 parts, subtracting
14 parts, a motion is gene-

00:52:44.800 --> 00:52:52.019
rated of 8 parts towards the contrary way.
But if the bodies were made both

00:52:52.019 --> 00:52:57.720
to move towards the same way, A, the swifter,
with 14 parts of motion, B,

00:52:57.720 --> 00:53:05.710
the slower, with 5, and after reflection A
went on with 5, B likewise went on

00:53:05.710 --> 00:53:14.119
with 14 parts; g parts being transferred from
A to B. And so in other cases.

00:53:14.119 --> 00:53:19.000
By the meeting and collision of bodies, the
quantity of motion, obtained

00:53:19.000 --> 00:53:23.950
from the sum of the motions directed towards
the same way, or from the

00:53:23.950 --> 00:53:29.200
difference of those that were directed towards
contrary ways, was never

00:53:29.200 --> 00:53:34.390
changed. For the error of an inch or two in
measures may be easily ascribed

00:53:34.390 --> 00:53:46.770
to the difficulty of executing everything
with accuracy. It was not easy to

00:53:46.770 --> 00:53:54.859
let go the two pendulumss so exactly together
that the bodies should impinge

00:53:54.859 --> 00:54:03.800
one upon the other in the lowermost place
AB; nor to mark the places s,

00:54:03.800 --> 00:54:10.130
and k, to which the bodies ascended after
impact. Nay, and some errors,

00:54:10.130 --> 00:54:14.819
too, might have happened from the unequal
density of the parts of the

00:54:14.819 --> 00:54:20.460
pendulous bodies themselves, and from the
irregularity of the texture proceeding from

00:54:20.460 --> 00:54:21.920
other causes.

00:54:21.920 --> 00:54:27.640
But to prevent an objection that may perhaps
be alleged against the rule,

00:54:27.640 --> 00:54:32.730
for the proof of which this experiment was
made, as if this rule did suppose that the

00:54:32.730 --> 00:54:37.190
bodies were either absolutely hard, or at
least perfectly elastic

00:54:37.190 --> 00:54:43.859
(whereas no such bodies are to be found in
Nature), I must add, that the

00:54:43.859 --> 00:54:48.540
experiments we have been describing, by no
means depending upon that

00:54:48.540 --> 00:54:54.779
quality of hardness, do succeed as well in
soft as in hard bodies. For if the

00:54:54.779 --> 00:55:00.010
rule is to be tried in bodies not perfectly
hard, we are only to diminish the

00:55:00.010 --> 00:55:05.109
reflection in such a certain proportion as
the quantity of the elastic force

00:55:05.109 --> 00:55:12.529
requires. By the theory of Wren and Huygens,
bodies absolutely hard re-

00:55:12.529 --> 00:55:18.069
turn one from another with the same velocity
with which they meet. But

00:55:18.069 --> 00:55:24.880
this may be affirmed with more certainty of
bodies perfectly elastic. In

00:55:24.880 --> 00:55:29.599
bodies imperfectly elastic the velocity of
the return is to be diminished

00:55:29.599 --> 00:55:35.180
together with the elastic force; because that
force (except when the parts

00:55:35.180 --> 00:55:40.630
of bodies are bruised by their impact, or
suffer some such extension as hap-

00:55:40.630 --> 00:55:46.290
pens under the strokes of a hammer) is (as
far as I can perceive) certain

00:55:46.290 --> 00:55:52.279
and determined, and makes the bodies to return
one from the other with

00:55:52.279 --> 00:55:58.510
a relative velocity, which is in a given ratio
to that relative velocity with

00:55:58.510 --> 00:56:05.430
which they met. This I tried in balls of wool,
made up tightly, and strongly

00:56:05.430 --> 00:56:11.589
compressed. For, first, by letting go the
pendulous bodies, and measuring

00:56:11.589 --> 00:56:17.819
their reflection, | determined the quantity
of their elastic force; and then,

00:56:17.819 --> 00:56:22.190
according to this force, estimated the reflections
that ought to happen in

00:56:22.190 --> 00:56:28.420
other cases of impact. And with this computation
other experiments made

00:56:28.420 --> 00:56:34.500
afterwards did accordingly agree; the balls
always receding one from the

00:56:34.500 --> 00:56:39.950
other with a relative velocity, which was
to the relative velocity with which

00:56:39.950 --> 00:56:47.240
they met as about 5 to g. Balls of steel returned
with almost the same

00:56:47.240 --> 00:56:52.970
velocity; those of cork with a velocity something
less; but in balls of glass

00:56:52.970 --> 00:57:01.359
the proportion was as about 15 to 16. And
thus the third Law, so far as it

00:57:01.359 --> 00:57:08.599
regards percussions and reflections, is proved
by a theory exactly agreeing

00:57:08.599 --> 00:57:10.470
with experience.

00:57:10.470 --> 00:57:16.441
In attractions, I briefly demonstrate the
thing after this manner. Suppose

00:57:16.441 --> 00:57:23.609
an obstacle is interposed to hinder the meeting
of any two bodies A, B,

00:57:23.609 --> 00:57:30.960
attracting one the other: then if either body,
as A, is more attracted towards

00:57:30.960 --> 00:57:36.549
the other body B, than that other body B is
towards the first body A, the

00:57:36.549 --> 00:57:41.940
obstacle will be more strongly urged by the
pressure of the body A than by

00:57:41.940 --> 00:57:47.230
the pressure of the body B, and therefore
will not remain in equilibrium:

00:57:47.230 --> 00:57:52.109
but the stronger pressure will prevail, and
will make the system of the two

00:57:52.109 --> 00:57:56.869
bodies, together with the obstacle, to move
directly towards the parts on

00:57:56.869 --> 00:58:03.970
which B lies; and in free spaces, to go forwards
in infinitum with a motion

00:58:03.970 --> 00:58:11.150
continually accelerated; which is absurd and
contrary to the first Law. For,

00:58:11.150 --> 00:58:16.980
by the first Law, the system ought to continue
in its state of rest, or of

00:58:16.980 --> 00:58:21.799
moving uniformly forwards in a right line;
and therefore the bodies must

00:58:21.799 --> 00:58:28.260
equally press the obstacle, and be equally
attracted one by the other. I made

00:58:28.260 --> 00:58:34.039
the experiment on the loadstone and iron.
If these, placed apart in proper

00:58:34.039 --> 00:58:40.859
vessels, are made to float by one another
in standing water, neither of them

00:58:40.859 --> 00:58:47.089
will propel the other; but, by being equally
attracted, they will sustain each

00:58:47.089 --> 00:58:51.410
other’s pressure, and rest at last in an
equilibrium.

00:58:51.410 --> 00:58:57.089
So the gravitation between the earth and its
parts is mutual. Let the earth

00:58:57.089 --> 00:59:07.690
FI be cut by any plane EG into two parts EGF
and EGI, and their weights

00:59:07.690 --> 00:59:15.119
one towards the other will be mutually equal.
For if by another plane HK,

00:59:15.119 --> 00:59:23.819
parallel to the former EG, the greater part
EGI is cut into two parts EGKH

00:59:23.819 --> 00:59:35.960
and HKI, whereof HKI is equal to the part
EFG, first cut off, it is evident that the

00:59:35.960 --> 00:59:41.519
middle
part EGKH will have no propension by its

00:59:41.519 --> 00:59:47.569
proper weight towards either side, but will
hang as it were, and rest in an equilibrium

00:59:47.569 --> 00:59:55.619
between both. But the one extreme part HKI
will with its whole weight bear upon and

00:59:55.619 --> 01:00:02.640
press the middle part towards the other extreme
part EGF; and therefore the force with

01:00:02.640 --> 01:00:11.160
which EGI, the sum of the parts HKI and EGKH,
tends towards the third

01:00:11.160 --> 01:00:18.329
part EGF, is equal to the weight of the part
HK], that is, to the weight of

01:00:18.329 --> 01:00:26.490
the third part EGF. And therefore the weights
of the two parts EGI and

01:00:26.490 --> 01:00:33.289
EGF, one towards the other, are equal, as
I was to prove. And indeed if those

01:00:33.289 --> 01:00:38.700
weights were not equal, the whole earth floating
in the nonresisting ether

01:00:38.700 --> 01:00:43.490
would give way to the greater weight, and,
retiring from it, would be carried

01:00:43.490 --> 01:00:52.680
off in infinitum.

01:00:52.680 --> 01:00:58.050
And as those bodies are equipollent in the
impact and reflection, whose

01:00:58.050 --> 01:01:06.499
velocities are inversely as their innate forces,
so in the use of mechanic instruments those

01:01:06.499 --> 01:01:11.339
agents are equipollent, and mutually sustain
each the contrary

01:01:11.339 --> 01:01:17.710
pressure of the other, whose velocities, estimated
according to the determination of the forces,

01:01:17.710 --> 01:01:20.630
are inversely as the forces.

01:01:20.630 --> 01:01:27.309
So those weights are of equal force to move
the arms of a balance, which

01:01:27.309 --> 01:01:33.039
during the play of the balance are inversely
as their velocities upwards and

01:01:33.039 --> 01:01:39.400
downwards; that is, if the ascent or descent
is direct, those weights are of

01:01:39.400 --> 01:01:45.839
equal force, which are inversely as the distances
of the points at which they

01:01:45.839 --> 01:01:51.140
are suspended from the axis of the balance;
but if they are turned aside by

01:01:51.140 --> 01:01:56.530
the interposition of oblique planes, or other
obstacles, and made to ascend

01:01:56.530 --> 01:02:03.210
or descend obliquely, those bodies will be
equipollent, which are inversely

01:02:03.210 --> 01:02:09.930
as the heights of their ascent and descent
taken according to the perpen-

01:02:09.930 --> 01:02:16.019
dicular; and that on account of the determination
of gravity downwards.

01:02:16.019 --> 01:02:21.109
And in like manner in the pulley, or in a
combination of pulleys, the

01:02:21.109 --> 01:02:26.640
force of a hand drawing the rope directly,
which is to the weight, whether

01:02:26.640 --> 01:02:32.140
ascending directly or obliquely, as the velocity
of the perpendicular ascent

01:02:32.140 --> 01:02:37.180
of the weight to the velocity of the hand
that draws the rope, will sustain

01:02:37.180 --> 01:02:39.960
the weight.

01:02:39.960 --> 01:02:44.849
In clocks and such like instruments, made
up from a combination of

01:02:44.849 --> 01:02:50.220
wheels, the contrary forces that promote and
impede the motion of the

01:02:50.220 --> 01:02:54.819
wheels, if they are inversely as the velocities
of the parts of the wheel on

01:02:54.819 --> 01:02:59.529
which they are impressed, will mutually sustain
each other.

01:02:59.529 --> 01:03:04.789
The force of the screw to press a body is
to the force of the hand that

01:03:04.789 --> 01:03:10.759
turns the handles by which it is moved as
the circular velocity of the handle

01:03:10.759 --> 01:03:15.950
in that part where it is impelled by the hand
is to the progressive velocity

01:03:15.950 --> 01:03:20.200
of the screw towards the pressed body.

01:03:20.200 --> 01:03:24.880
The forces by which the wedge presses or drives
the two parts of the

01:03:24.880 --> 01:03:30.410
wood it cleaves are to the force of the mallet
upon the wedge as the progress

01:03:30.410 --> 01:03:34.950
of the wedge in the direction of the force
impressed upon it by the mallet

01:03:34.950 --> 01:03:40.180
is to the velocity with which the parts of
the wood yield to the wedge, in

01:03:40.180 --> 01:03:45.829
the direction of lines perpendicular to the
sides of the wedge. And the like

01:03:45.829 --> 01:03:49.299
account is to be given of all machines.

01:03:49.299 --> 01:03:56.480
The power and use of machines consist only
in this, that by diminishing

01:03:56.480 --> 01:04:02.200
the velocity we may augment the force, and
the contrary; from whence, in

01:04:02.200 --> 01:04:08.559
all sorts of proper machines, we have the
solution of this problem: To move

01:04:08.559 --> 01:04:15.559
a given weight with a given power, or with
a given force to overcome any

01:04:15.559 --> 01:04:21.319
other given resistance. For if machines are
so contrived that the velocities

01:04:21.319 --> 01:04:28.569
of the agent and resistant are inversely as
their forces, the agent will just

01:04:28.569 --> 01:04:34.249
sustain the resistant, but with a greater
disparity of velocity will overcome

01:04:34.249 --> 01:04:41.560
it. So that if the disparity of velocities
is so great as to overcome all that

01:04:41.560 --> 01:04:46.549
resistance which commonly arises either from
the friction of contiguous

01:04:46.549 --> 01:04:52.069
bodies as they slide by one another, or from
the cohesion of continuous

01:04:52.069 --> 01:04:58.329
bodies that are to be separated, or from the
weights of bodies to be raised,

01:04:58.329 --> 01:05:03.650
the excess of the force remaining, after all
those resistances are overcome,

01:05:03.650 --> 01:05:10.230
will produce an acceleration of motion proportional
thereto, as well in the

01:05:10.230 --> 01:05:17.890
parts of the machine as in the resisting body.
But to treat of mechanics is not my present

01:05:17.890 --> 01:05:22.980
business. | was aiming only to show by those
examples the

01:05:22.980 --> 01:05:29.130
great extent and certainty of the third Law
of Motion. For if we estimate

01:05:29.130 --> 01:05:34.609
the action of the agent from the product of
its force and velocity, and like-

01:05:34.609 --> 01:05:39.670
wise the reaction of the impediment from the
product of the velocities of its

01:05:39.670 --> 01:05:44.239
several parts, and the forces of resistance
arising from the friction, cohesion,

01:05:44.239 --> 01:05:50.869
weight, and acceleration of those parts, the
action and reaction in the use

01:05:50.869 --> 01:05:57.880
of all sorts of machines will be found always
equal to one another. And

01:05:57.880 --> 01:06:02.550
so far as the action is propagated by the
intervening instruments, and at

01:06:02.550 --> 01:06:08.730
last impressed upon the resisting body, the
ultimate action will be always

01:06:08.730 --> 01:06:13.119
contrary to the reaction.

